From 61880398ad678385c3601d0321695cb3f601d550 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 13:19:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] x86-64: widen Clz/Ctz/Popcount results through the
 unsigned path (PF-16b)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`(long)__builtin_clz(x)` emitted lzcntl + cltq — but lzcntl writes
%eax, which zeroes bits 32..63 on x86-64, and the result is <= 32, so
bits 31..63 are provably zero: the cltq sign-extension is bit-identical
to the zero-extension the hardware already performed. Same for ctz,
popcount, and the 8/16-bit forms (movsbq/movswq on non-negative bytes
vs movzbl/movzwl).

Mechanism: the prologue records Clz/Ctz/Popcount destinations of at
most 32-bit types in bitop_nonneg_values (per-definition — values
DERIVED from a bitop result can wrap negative and are deliberately not
propagated). Widening casts of such values to 64-bit reclassify
from_ty to its unsigned peer, so every downstream path (register-
direct, slot, accumulator) emits the cheap zero form; a MachInst gate
routes the same shapes to the mature emitter, which MI would otherwise
sign-extend eagerly. The acc-residency elision (dropping movl
%eax,%eax when the value is provably canonical in %rax) was considered
and REJECTED: the accumulator cache's residency invariant does not
prove rax[63:32]==0 for arbitrary 32-bit residents, and the elision
would rest on an unproven assumption — 3 bytes are not worth it.

Validated: ffs/clz/popcount kernels lose the cltq (bit-identical
results); lib gate 1302/0 under -D warnings; x86-64 corpus 966/0;
godbolt census unchanged at 6258/5773 (the benchmark set contains no
clz-widen shape — the win is on the ffs1-class gap recorded in the
backlog).
